            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's entry closely aligns with the established consensus regarding the symbolic meaning of 'night' as spiritual darkness, the concealment of evil deeds, and the eschatological age preceding the Day of the Lord. The provided texts (1 Thessalonians 5, John 3:19, Romans 13:12) thoroughly support both the individual moral dimension (sleep, hidden sin) and the macro-scale epochal dimension (the present age before the Kingdom).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Night symbolizes the absence of Light/Day (visible obedience), characterized by sleep, death, and the concealment of sinful deeds.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"At the largest scale, it is the period from the fall of man until the Kingdom of God begins on earth.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter's typological application of the ninth plague of darkness as a historical staging of 'two houses under one sky' (the covenant home lit vs. the lawless land dark) is an interesting extension, but it is not printed in the glossary entry and is thus excluded from the scope.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Night primarily symbolizes general moral and spiritual darkness. Its eschatological use (as the present evil age) is merely one secondary, contextual application in Romans 13, and describing it strictly as the epoch 'from the fall of man until the Kingdom' over-systematizes a flexible metaphor.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"Night is spiritual and moral darkness, varying in scope from individual sin to times of trial, without inherently denoting a specific historical epoch spanning from the Fall to the Kingdom.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_NARROWER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that the overarching historical epoch (from the fall to the Kingdom) is definitively built into the symbol's primary biblical definition, viewing it instead as a contextual metaphor.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"In texts like Psalm 30:5, night is a temporary season of trial, not the entire era from the fall.\",\n    \"Romans 13:12 uses 'night' for the present age metaphorically, but this doesn't strictly lock the symbol's definition to a timeline starting at the fall of man.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"The book seamlessly integrates the individual moral application (John 3, 1 Thess 5) with the macro-eschatological application (Rom 13:12) under a single cohesive framework.\",\n    \"By defining Light as visible obedience/righteous works, Night logically stands as its direct foil—the concealment of evil (John 3:19) and the spiritually asleep condition of the present age (1 Thess 5:7).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Avoids needing to date the 'start' of the metaphorical night specifically to the fall of man, allowing for broader, looser poetic applications.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"1 Thessalonians 5:5-7\",\n    \"Romans 13:12\",\n    \"John 3:19-20\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
